      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 56-year old man presented with increasing angina pectoris. Coronary angiogram showed a triple-vessel disease, with significant lesions on the main stem, on an obtuse marginal branch of the circumflex coronary artery (Cx), on the right coronary artery (RCA), and a proximal occlusion of the left anterior descending artery (LAD). A hybrid procedure was decided, with a beating heart totally endoscopic double vessel coronary artery bypass grafting (Double BHTECAB) on the LAD and the Cx, with the use of a four-arm robotic device, and a stent placement into the RCA in a second step. Both procedures went uneventfully, and the patient is fully asymptomatic 15 months after the procedure."},
